url: 'https://github.com/J-Davidson01/realistic-energy'
abstract: >-
  The increasing reliance on software-driven systems
  contributes to increased energy

  consumption, placing increasing pressure on energy grids
  and energy infrastructure.

  In large-scale systems, even marginal improvements in
  energy efficiency can translate

  into substantial reductions in total energy demand. As
  software is the anchor of these

  systems, its design and implementation have a direct
  impact on energy usage. In

  particular, programming languages can significantly
  influence the energy efficiency of

  applications.

  Existing studies evaluating programming language energy
  efficiency often rely on

  small synthetic microbenchmarks or isolated case studies,
  which may not capture how

  applications behave across CPU-, memory-, and I/O-bound
  workloads and their com-

  bined execution. As a result, the validity of these
  findings remains limited and there

  is no widely accepted benchmark suite that enables
  reproducible and comparative

  evaluation across such workload types.

  This thesis addresses this gap by implementing and
  evaluating a reproducible

  benchmark suite composed of CPU-, memory-, and I/O-bound
  workloads, imple-

  mented across multiple programming languages, including
  compiled, just-in-time (JIT)

  compiled, and interpreted languages. The proposed
  methodology evaluates these

  workloads both in isolation and as a composed end-to-end
  data processing pipeline,

  covering CPU computation, memory access, and I/O
  operations.

  - High-level findings
keywords:
  - Software benchmarking
  - Programming language execution models
  - Runtime environments
  - Data processing pipelines
  - Optimization techniques
